def format_docs(docs):
    return "\n\n".join(doc.page_content for doc in docs)

document_text = """
Leaving home at 7 a.m. to commute, working in front of a computer all day, and returning home at 8 p.m. — many office workers know the advice to “exercise for at least 30 minutes a day,” but the problem is time. Most likely, many professionals share this sentiment.

Recent exercise medicine studies, however, offer a new message to such individuals: move intensely enough to catch your breath several times a day, even if briefly. For example, take the stairs quickly instead of the elevator, speed up on uphill commutes, or walk briskly for a minute after lunch before returning to the office.

A study recently published in the international journal *Cell Reports Medicine* explained the reason at the molecular level. Researchers divided 19 young, healthy men into two groups: one cycled at moderate intensity for 90 minutes, while the other cycled all-out for 30 seconds six times (3 minutes total) with 4-minute intervals. They analyzed changes in blood proteins after exercise.

Among 2,884 plasma proteins measured, the high-intensity group showed increases in 714 proteins. These included growth hormones and substances related to vascular function, tissue remodeling, and fat metabolism. In contrast, only 7 proteins changed significantly in the moderate-intensity group.

Exercise doesn’t just move muscles. The heart pumps more blood, lungs increase oxygen supply, the liver releases energy, and fat tissue sends out fatty acids. Blood vessels respond to blood flow changes. These organs exchange signals through proteins and metabolites released into the blood during exercise.

The moment exercise begins, a group chat opens in the body: “Muscles are using a lot of energy.” “Supply fat.” “Increase blood flow.” “Bring more glucose.” “Expand blood vessels.” Muscles, fat, liver, blood vessels, and the brain exchange signals. Even short, intense exercise stimulates all organs simultaneously.

High-intensity exercise is an energy emergency at the cellular level. Mitochondria, the muscle’s power plants, are activated to boost energy production. Blood glucose is rapidly consumed. Sprinting or climbing stairs quickly makes muscles actively use blood glucose, improving insulin sensitivity.

The body doesn’t immediately return to normal after intense exercise. It consumes additional oxygen and energy to stabilize heart rate, breathing, body temperature, and replenish spent energy. This prolonged post-exercise effect, called excess post-exercise oxygen consumption, commonly occurs after high-intensity workouts.

Most people can’t sprint all day. Hence, the concept of “intermittent high-intensity physical activity in daily life” emerged. It refers to brief, breathless movements like running to catch a late bus, climbing stairs quickly, or power-walking uphill.

A study published in *Nature Medicine* tracked 25,241 adults (average age 62) without regular exercise using wearable devices for about seven years. Those who engaged in an average of 4.4 minutes of daily high-intensity activity had 26–30% lower overall mortality risk and 32–34% lower cardiovascular mortality risk compared to those who did none. The body recognizes short, intense movements as exercise.

Rethink physical activity strategies. Walk, cycle, and do strength training regularly, but incorporate short, high-intensity intervals suited to your health. In daily life, climb a few flights of stairs quickly or walk briskly for 1–2 minutes during a stroll. Even without dedicated exercise time, embed “breathless moments” throughout the day. However, those with hypertension, diabetes, coronary artery disease, or symptoms like chest pain, severe shortness of breath, or dizziness during exercise should be cautious.

Shift the unit of exercise from “30 minutes” or “1 hour” to 30 seconds, 1 minute, or a few flights of stairs. The new standard is: “How many times did you move intensely enough to catch your breath today?” If time is limited, move intensely, even briefly.
"""